WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.049
Preparing food takes

00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:12.554
an amazing amount of attention to detail.

00:00:13.596 --> 00:00:16.516
Food is one of the great equalizers.

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.394
It offers an incredible opportunity

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:22.856
for laughter, for deep discussion,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.859
contemplation, problem solving.

00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:30.572
When I am cooking,

00:00:30.572 --> 00:00:35.618
the goal is to have someone eat
and have their senses awakened

00:00:36.161 --> 00:00:40.206
and take them on a journey to rediscover

00:00:40.331 --> 00:00:50.216
the dish, each time they take a bite.

00:00:51.259 --> 00:00:53.470
Hi, my name's Erick Williams.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.056
I am the chef and owner of Virtue

00:00:56.056 --> 00:01:00.602
Restaurant, Daisy's Po'Boy and Tavern,
Mustard Seed Take Out Kitchen,

00:01:00.727 --> 00:01:04.981
and Top This Mac and Cheese.

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:10.528
What else we got to add to that gumbo?

00:01:10.528 --> 00:01:13.239
It’s roux? It just needs to be stirred?

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:13.823
Yes sir.

00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:23.708
My man.

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:29.672
I decided to invite friends from the chefs
community into my home and the tradition

00:01:29.714 --> 00:01:35.220
of a red beans and rice dinner
traditionally found in New Orleans.

00:01:36.346 --> 00:01:38.890
I grilled chicken,

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.268
ash roasted rutabaga, roasted chicken.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:45.980
Additionally,
I seared American red snapper.

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:51.194
I did a lima bean and mustard green dish

00:01:51.653 --> 00:02:03.123
alongside of that with coconut rice.

00:02:06.751 --> 00:02:09.337
When I think back to what I saw

00:02:09.337 --> 00:02:13.049
most as a kid, it was Southern food.

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:16.094
I feel very connected

00:02:16.094 --> 00:02:19.305
to that space when I'm cooking.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.516
I look to pay a lot of homage

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:24.477
to those that cooked before me

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.730
to think “who was the person

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.692
that discovered collard greens and thought

00:02:31.192 --> 00:02:34.988
to cook them in a way that was healing

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:46.666
and in a way that will provide
a way of survival?” The first thing I do

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:50.670
when preparing chicken on the grill is
I generally brine it for a day.

00:02:51.212 --> 00:02:53.798
I chose to use a Tabasco brine.

00:02:54.465 --> 00:02:57.510
It allows me to season
the chicken really evenly.

00:02:58.136 --> 00:03:01.514
I take the backbone out
because when grilling chicken,

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:05.143
I like to have a flat surface,
much like people would do

00:03:05.143 --> 00:03:08.438
if they were doing brick chicken.

00:03:08.438 --> 00:03:10.481
Bring the chicken up to room temperature.

00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:13.902
Then I move into lighting the grill,

00:03:15.486 --> 00:03:16.487
cooking logs

00:03:16.487 --> 00:03:25.288
down to coals.

00:03:25.288 --> 00:03:28.666
We brush the chicken with oil,

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:32.670
seasoned it lightly with salt and pepper

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:36.966
and I apply the chicken on to the grill,

00:03:37.258 --> 00:03:41.221
breast and leg side down.

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:44.265
I was really lucky to get beautiful

00:03:44.641 --> 00:03:48.228
American red snapper.

00:03:48.228 --> 00:03:51.981
I prepared the snapper
by filleting it off of the bone.

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:55.443
There's almost always still a few scales

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:59.572
and usually the most
unlikely thing for me to do at home.

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:01.824
But it's one of those necessary evils.

00:04:02.242 --> 00:04:05.161
And the reason it's unlikely for me
to do at home is because the scales

00:04:05.161 --> 00:04:08.957
get everywhere.

00:04:08.957 --> 00:04:14.587
Once the snappers filleted
and I've removed all visible scales,

00:04:14.712 --> 00:04:18.007
I check the fillets, make sure that
there are no remaining bones,

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:22.804
and if so, I use fish tweezers
to remove those bones.

00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:26.140
And then I portion the snapper

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:29.102
to basically a pretty uniform size

00:04:29.727 --> 00:04:33.564
for a fillet
that doesn't really want to be uniform.

00:04:33.564 --> 00:04:37.110
I’d season them with salt, pepper,

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:41.739
sear them to get the skin crisp
and serve them

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:54.419
garnished with scallions.

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:57.839
As a child, I was fun loving.

00:04:58.006 --> 00:05:00.967
I was energetic, curious.

00:05:01.718 --> 00:05:06.639
I lived in a home that was safe, that was
encouraging, that contained rigor.

00:05:08.391 --> 00:05:11.144
I was
working in kitchens as a means to an end,

00:05:11.352 --> 00:05:14.814
and I had the opportunity
to meet a chef, Michael Kornick.

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:18.818
And he was so passionate about food,
it became infectious.

00:05:19.861 --> 00:05:22.030
I worked at MK for 20

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:30.872
years.

00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:33.916
Rutabagas
always have a wax coating on them.

00:05:34.792 --> 00:05:37.128
And so I take the rutabaga

00:05:37.128 --> 00:05:41.632
and I lay them
directly on top of the coals

00:05:41.924 --> 00:05:45.261
and they look like charcoal
by the time they're done.

00:05:47.096 --> 00:05:49.057
If I was to describe a rutabaga,

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:51.559
I would describe it
as what a turnip wants to be.

00:05:52.268 --> 00:05:57.982
Rutabagas
have a natural sweetness to them.

00:05:57.982 --> 00:06:01.694
The rutabagas
they're dressed with sunflower seeds,

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.281
arugula, midnight

00:06:05.281 --> 00:06:08.117
moon cheese, which is a firm

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:14.791
goat cheese.

00:06:14.791 --> 00:06:17.210
I generally soak my beans overnight.

00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:21.756
In this case I was using dried lima beans.

00:06:21.756 --> 00:06:25.551
I started with
diced onion, red bell pepper

00:06:27.095 --> 00:06:30.473
and sliced garlic.

00:06:30.473 --> 00:06:34.936
I sweat the onions, the garlic,

00:06:34.936 --> 00:06:37.480
then I add the red bell peppers.

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:41.109
At that point I stir the beans in

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:44.529
and I add turkey stock.

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:47.990
I simmer the beans

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.535
until they're about 80% done.

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:54.372
At that point, I add enough mustard

00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:57.875
greens where there is about a 60%

00:06:58.126 --> 00:07:01.838
ratio of mustard greens to beans, once

00:07:01.838 --> 00:07:13.516
they wilt.

00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:15.268
Hi everyone How are you doing Chef?

00:07:15.268 --> 00:07:19.397
Virtue, Daisy’s,

00:07:20.356 --> 00:07:23.860
Mustard Seed Kitchen and now Top
This Mac and Cheese,

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:27.071
were all designed to fill a void.

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:30.366
I strongly believed that the world

00:07:30.366 --> 00:07:33.411
was craving kindness
and it continues to crave kindness.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:36.247
And we wanted to have a space

00:07:36.914 --> 00:07:39.083
that is considered safe space,

00:07:40.001 --> 00:07:44.297
that is culturally alive and vibrant,

00:07:45.548 --> 00:07:48.301
that tells the stories

00:07:48.301 --> 00:07:51.971
in the narrative
of the black experience through food

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:54.599
and music

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:57.810
and textures and art.

00:07:58.936 --> 00:08:02.982
And we wanted to harness that
in one space.

00:08:03.816 --> 00:08:05.568
Hello, Welcome to Daisy’s
Po-Boy and Tavern.

00:08:05.568 --> 00:08:06.736
How are you doing?

00:08:07.945 --> 00:08:08.946
I’m good. How are you?

00:08:08.946 --> 00:08:11.157
I’m great. I’m great.
How are you enjoying your sandwich?

00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:13.618
Good, did you make this?

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:18.623
We want places that feel inclusive,

00:08:19.749 --> 00:08:23.127
it's just surrounded by black culture.

00:08:23.127 --> 00:08:25.004
That's what sets the table.

00:08:25.004 --> 00:08:26.255
I appreciate you, man.

00:08:26.255 --> 00:08:27.340
I didn’t know it was Black owned.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.591
I appreciate ya’ll man.

00:08:28.591 --> 00:08:29.509
Thank you.

00:08:39.852 --> 00:08:44.982
I took a warm pan,
I sweat diced sweet potatoes added

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:47.652
par cooked Carolina gold rice

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:52.281
and I moisten that rice with coconut milk.

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:57.870
Fold it in the coconut
milk, loosen the rice with a little bit

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:01.958
of the turkey stock that we have reserved
and finish it

00:09:01.958 --> 00:09:08.464
with salt, pepper.

00:09:08.464 --> 00:09:11.592
I thought this particular meal
was an incredible opportunity

00:09:11.926 --> 00:09:14.345
for me to invite friends

00:09:14.971 --> 00:09:16.973
from the chefs community into my home.

00:09:17.807 --> 00:09:21.102
To just let our hair down,
get a chance to connect.

00:09:22.603 --> 00:09:23.896
You know, I think that

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:26.983
people that are at your dinner
table should be people that you respect.

00:09:27.942 --> 00:09:30.528
I think that's part of the experience.

00:09:30.528 --> 00:09:32.613
That's grandma dish, right there.

00:09:36.242 --> 00:09:37.243
Like, really,

00:09:37.243 --> 00:09:39.954
how many times
do we get a chance to do this Never.

00:09:40.329 --> 00:09:42.999
Right. That's the other part.

00:09:42.999 --> 00:09:45.459
So fellowship is important.

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:48.671
We take a lot of pride in making sure

00:09:48.671 --> 00:09:53.843
that we leave the industry
better than it was when we found it.

00:09:53.843 --> 00:09:58.347
And part of our contribution
is making sure that young cooks can see

00:09:58.347 --> 00:10:02.977
chefs that look like them and owners
and operators that look like them.

00:10:02.977 --> 00:10:07.189
And an effort
for us all to believe that, you know,

00:10:07.565 --> 00:10:12.236
the world is not one dimensional,
it's not made for some and not others.

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:16.657
And that that the
stigmas are put behind us.

00:10:17.617 --> 00:10:20.328
And that's important to me.

